  "notes": "The Hypnotize board with one thing turned up: the research says her records lean on a busier hat and cowbell layer to match a faster syllable rate, so `hihat.fillDensity` and `percs.densityPerBar` are the highest in the classic half of this batch while everything under them stays the group's. Harmony is a single-chord i vamp with a ♭II or VI stab; `impliedByRiff` is set for that reason and the chord part's variety comes from `chordDurationBeats` and a wide voicing register. The one place the parent is deliberately softened is `extensions` — the research notes the 2001 major-label record lifting toward brighter minor-7 voicings for radio, so sevenths reach 18% where the parent sits at 7%. Hooks are chanted in a 3–4 semitone band with call-and-response answers from the posse, which is what `motifPitchCount` and the answer-lick-weighted countermelody author. ⚠ `medium`: the research rates it medium and is explicit that **the drum and tempo parameters are inherited from the documented Hypnotize board rather than sourced for her individually** — `session.bpm`, `drums.kick` and `drums.snare` are the inferred ones. What is hers by description is the busier hat/cowbell layer, the narrow chant band, and the radio-era seventh colour. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 40 to 39 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 40 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
